  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}(A)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $A,$ we actually multiply each row of $A$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093810/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
